A||||9||drednot||clutch problems?? DOH~! help??!||||11||
Z||000000||drednot||05-11-2001||07:06 PM||||Lately I have been having major problems getting into first and reverse (clutch not fully disengaging).  First I bled out the system.  No help what so ever, and yes I did it right.  Then I find out about the GM design flaw in the clutch pedal.  So I get a new one from GM.  I just swapped out the old one about a week ago.  Very little improvement.  I compared the old clutch pedal to the new one, and I sure don't see any difference (except weight).  What Is weird now, is that when I first start the car, the clutch works great, goes into gear easy with no grinding.  after it gets hot (about ten minutes)  the thing is very hard to get into first, and reverse just grinds.  what the hell!!  I tried everything.  could this just be a clutch problem?  How much should it run me to get the clutch replaced?  I got quoted $800 at least from one shop.  I just don't think the car is even worth that much at this point.  A clutch should not total a car!  It can not be that badly designed that it would cost $800 to replace the thing.  btw- I have an 1985 4 cyl 5 speed.  what do you all think?||||reg||11
Z||000001||drednot||05-12-2001||12:05 PM||||Thanks for all the replies.  in all, 0.||||reg||9
Z||000002||mrgone||05-12-2001||12:46 PM||||im have that same prob.<P>but it doesnt happen when i first start the car, only when ive been going for awhile, <P>so to get it into reverse someone here told me to put in into 2nd before, and you are only suppsed to put it into first when youre stopped so that solved my first gear problem..<P>try it and see if it helps||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||05-12-2001||02:09 PM||||Sorry we're not all experts in clutches...have you tried a search for other clutch topics, yours seems like a fairly common problem (both my fieros have had 1st gear issues)<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/daytona1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnt671||05-12-2001||04:18 PM||||I'm also not a clutch xpert, but it seems that if it works sometimes but not always you most likely have something wrong in the master cyl. or slave cyl. If it works good when cold but not hot, check the boiling temp on your brake fluid and make sure you are using is good.  I just changed to syn. fluid and slipped as I was bleeding the old fluid out and had a heck of a time getting it all out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Huck||05-12-2001||07:44 PM||||I was having somewhat the same problem up until this afternoon at about 3:30 ....  sitting at a stoplight and had a hard time getting it into first, so I pushed the clutch pedel harder (thinking my floormat may have been stopping it from going its full movement) and all of a sudden pop! then it stalled...<BR>Pushed it out of the middle of the intersection and into a harveys driveway... turns out I blew my slave cylindar.<P>Just got home now... driving without a clutch is a pain in the ass.  especially at stop signs and lights, when I had to turn the car off then start it when it was in 1st to get going again.. I hate having to do that.<P>now, my question.. can I get a slave cylindar off of any fiero, or are they engine/year specific.  I have an 87 GT 5spd.<P>thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000006||sjp777||05-12-2001||08:14 PM||||Have someone sit in the car and push the pedal while you look at the slave. If the slave is not moving the release arm at least 1" to an 1 1/2", you have either a bad master or a bad slave. If that's the case, you can bleed until you get an eye tick and it won't do no good. Also, check the release lever itself carefully. The 86's and earlier were stamped steel instead of forged alloy. The steel ones crack where it clamps on the release shaft and then it just flexes without really turning the shaft.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||05-13-2001||01:55 PM||||This is just a basic thought but when I first got my Fiero, I noticed that I had to make and effort to make sure my toes on my left foot angled to the right when I depressed the clutch.  If I didn't do this I couldn't push the pedal down far enough to release and had problems.  When you sit in the car your left foot tends to angle to the left and I kept hitting my toes on the foot rest.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chance||05-14-2001||04:32 AM|||| I was having clutch problems for so long, that I thought I would never have a normal car again! You MAY want to make sure the shift cables are properly adjusted.<BR> I replaced the slave cyl on it (I know everyone will scream but I got my slave from AutoZone for 30 some bucks a few months ago) I just checked their site, but now they just have em listed for 67.99.  Anyway, all that I done, didn't do any good really...  I FINALLY took it in to a shop that told me they would look at it. (I had a new clutch put in less then 3 years ago, closer to 2, BUT this was after it sat at my parents for almost a year before I admited defeat!  They tore it apart, and found the clutch fork was messed up, and the pressure plate had some broken fingers.  &lt;SIGH&gt;  so I told him to go ahead and fix it.  I needed a new Master because it was leakey, and new clutch fork... all in all, they charged me 800 bucks but the place I took it guarenteed the parts and labor for a year, or 12,000 miles... and the shop was great about it, told me "I don't know why everyone else won't do Fiero clutches, they are not that hard to do"  but if you search some of the old post, you'll see I went through it all too, I just didn't think that the place that replaced the clutch the first time for 1200 <BR>work would be so shoddy!!!(found out that my buddy had took his porsche in there and it slipped off the lift and they tried to weasel out of it) Oh well... Hope u fix it, I drove around almost 6 or so months with no clutch (like Huck above) and it sucked.||||reg||4
Z||000009||Chance||05-14-2001||04:37 AM||||OH!!! and make sure, check the Master Cyl rod the attaches to your clutch pedal, the "hump" side goes UP <BR> when I got mine back, it was hump down) <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Chance (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||4
